<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberal Revolution of 1895</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Liberal Revolution (Ecuador)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liberal Revolution of 1895 in Ecuador began with a rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> on 5 June 1895 that proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eloy-alfaro">Eloy Alfaro</a> supreme chief. Liberal forces then fought their way into the Sierra and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, ending the conservative order. The liberal governments that followed separated church and state, secularised education and marriage, and completed the Guayaquil–Quito railway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1895.jpg" alt="Liberal Revolution of 1895" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: enciclopediadelecuador.com · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla-de-Chasqui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
